course: books
lesson: 90
title: Just So Stories, The Crab that Played with the Sea, part 1: the Eldest Magician and the sea that will not behave
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At the Very Beginning the Eldest Magician gets the Earth and the Sea ready and teaches each animal its «play»: the Elephant plays at being an Elephant, the Beaver at being a Beaver, and so on. While he talks to the Man, who has his little daughter on his shoulder, Pau Amma the Crab scuttles sideways into the sea to play alone. Only the little girl sees him go.
explain: The Magician walks round the world. In the North the Elephant's diggings become the Himalayas; in the East the Cow's eaten patch and her sitting-place become the Indian Desert and the Sahara; in the West the Beaver's dam becomes the Everglades; in the South the Turtle's flung sand becomes the islands of Malaysia and Indonesia.
explain: But the Man says the Sea is not obedient: once a day and once a night it rushes up the Perak river and floods his house, then runs out again and leaves only mud. The Magician says that is «a new and a bad play», and they set off in a canoe with the little girl to find out who is playing with the Sea.
explain: The sea runs out so fast that the canoe is pulled past Selangor, Malacca and Singapore to the island of Bintang. The Magician asks all the animals, who say «not us»; he asks the old Fisherman in the Moon, who spins a line to catch the world, and the Rat in the Moon, who bites the line. Neither is playing with the Sea.
fact: This tale is set «before the High and Far-Off Times», in «the Time of the Very Beginnings», even earlier than the other stories.
fact: The animals are called «All-the-Elephant-there-was», «All-the-Cow-there-was» and so on, because there was only one of each at the start.
fact: The Man says «Yes, I am too wise for this play; but see that you make all the Animals obedient to me.»
fact: Pau Amma the Crab, «next in the game», says «I will play my play alone in the deep waters, and I will never be obedient to this son of Adam.»
fact: «Kun?» means «Is this right?» and «Payah kun» means «That is quite right» in the story's own words.
fact: The Magician breathes on the animals' work: Elephant, North, the Himalayan Mountains; Cow, East, the Indian Desert and the Desert of Sahara; Beaver, West, the Everglades in Florida; Turtle, South, Borneo, Celebes, Sumatra, Java and the Malay Archipelago.
fact: The Man's trouble: «Once a day and once a night the Sea runs up the Perak river and drives the sweet-water back into the forest», and then «draws all the water after it, so that there is nothing left but mud, and my canoe is upset.»
fact: The Man takes his kris, «a curving, wavy dagger with a blade like a flame». The Perak river, Selangor, Malacca, Singapore and Bintang are real places in and near Malaysia.
fact: The Fisherman of the Moon is «spinning a fishing-line with which he hopes one day to catch the world», and the Rat of the Moon «always bites the old Fisherman's line as fast as it is made».
fact: Real life: the Himalayas were pushed up because the land of India has been moving north and crashing into Asia for about 50 million years; they are still rising a little every year.
fact: Real life: the Sahara was green, with lakes, hippos and grass, until about 5,000 to 6,000 years ago; it dried because of slow changes in the Earth's tilt and the rains, not because a cow sat there.
fact: Real life: the Everglades in Florida are a very wide, slow «river of grass» over flat land, made by rain and water flowing south, not by a beaver; there are no beavers in the Everglades.
fact: Real life: the dark patches on the Moon are wide plains of old cooled lava; people in different lands see a man, a rabbit or a woman in them. There is no Fisherman or Rat up there.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «skibidi» = a silly word kids shout, meaning nothing much; «wicked» (New England, USA) = very.
exercise: Who gets the Earth and the Sea ready at the Very Beginning? | The Eldest Magician | Nqong, the Big God, from his bath in the salt-pan | The Djinn of All Deserts | The Man
exercise: What does the Magician tell each animal to do? | Play at being itself | Work hard for the Man from morning till night | Find a home of its own | Stay well out of the sea
exercise: Why is it «All-the-Elephant-there-was»? | There was only one Elephant at the start | He was the biggest Elephant | He ate all the others | It is his family's long name, like Tegumai Bopsulai
exercise: Who comes up towards evening? | The Man, with his little girl-daughter on his shoulder | The Woman, with her baby in her arms and the Dog | The Crab | The Moon
exercise: What does the Man ask the Magician to do? | Make all the Animals obedient to him | Teach him a play of his own, like the Elephant's and the Beaver's | Give him a canoe | Make him a house
exercise: What does Pau Amma do while they talk? | Scuttles sideways into the sea | Hides under a stone on the beach until the play is over | Bites the Man's toe | Falls asleep
exercise: Who sees Pau Amma go? | Only the little girl-daughter | The Eldest Magician, out of the corner of his eye | Nobody at all | The Turtle
exercise: What does «Kun?» mean? | Is this right? | Thank you | Goodbye | Help!
exercise: In the North, what do the Elephant's diggings become? | The Himalayan Mountains | The Desert of Sahara and the Indian Desert | The Everglades of Florida | Borneo and the islands near it
exercise: The Cow licks up whole forests. What do her eaten patch and her sitting-place become? | The Indian Desert and the Sahara | The Himalayas and the Alps, the two highest mountain ranges | A great lake | The Everglades
exercise: Which animal's work becomes the Everglades in Florida? | The Beaver's dam | The Turtle's sand | The Cow's grassy bed | The Elephant's tusks
exercise: What does the Turtle's flung sand become? | Borneo, Java and other islands | The long white beaches of Florida, where the turtles lay their eggs | The Sahara | The Moon
exercise: What does the Man say the Sea does «once a day and once a night»? | Floods up the river, then runs out leaving mud | Freezes into ice so hard that it cracks his canoe in two and he cannot fish | Turns sweet | Sings
exercise: What does the Eldest Magician call the Sea's behaviour? | A new and a bad play | A good game that the Sea has played since the very beginning | A storm | The Man's fault
exercise: What is a kris? | A wavy dagger | A paddle carved from one piece of wood | A big fishing-net | A little canoe
exercise: Which way does the canoe go when the sea runs out? | Out past Selangor, Malacca and Singapore to Bintang | Up the Perak river, deep into the dark forest | Round in circles | Nowhere
exercise: What do all the beasts, birds and fishes answer? | Not one of us plays with the Sea | The Crab does, we saw him go into the deep water | The Moon does | We all do
exercise: What is the Fisherman of the Moon doing? | Spinning a line to catch the world | Catching silver fish in the sea of stars for his supper | Pulling the sea | Sleeping
exercise: What does the Rat of the Moon do? | Bites through the Fisherman's line | Eats the Moon, a little bit every night, till it is thin | Pulls the sea | Hides in the Fisherman's hat
exercise: Looking back: which earlier story had a Djinn who gave an animal something on its back? | How the Camel Got His Hump | The Elephant's Child | The Sing-Song of Old Man Kangaroo | The Beginning of the Armadillos
exercise: Looking back: which earlier story had a gentleman god, Nqong, in a bath in the salt-pan? | The Sing-Song of Old Man Kangaroo | How the Leopard Got His Spots | How the Whale Got His Throat | How the First Letter Was Written
exercise: Looking back: which other little girl in the book saw and spoke while grown-ups were busy? | Taffy | Teshumai | The Butterfly's wife | Mother Jaguar
test: Kofi: «the Magician saw the Crab run off but let him, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | Nobody saw him go except the little girl, who said nothing | Right, the Magician watched him go and laughed | The Man saw and chased him | The Turtle told on him
test: Why does Pau Amma run off? | He gets tired of waiting while the Magician talks to the Man | He is afraid that the Elephant will tread on him when it digs the mountains | He is hungry | He wants to see the Moon
test: «Is all the Sea obedient to you?» «No,» says the Man. What does the sea actually do, in real words? | It rises and falls twice or so a day, which we call tides | It boils | It freezes at night | It talks
test: What would happen if the Man's house were on a hill? | The flooding sea could not wet it | It would float away down the river to Bintang with the canoe | The crab would visit | The Moon would fall
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | The sea pulls the canoe out to Bintang | The Magician paddles hard all the way, with the Man and the girl asleep in the canoe | The Crab is caught at once | The Fisherman admits it
test: Ella from Boston: «wicked cool that a beaver made the Everglades.» (wicked = very) What is the real story? | Rain and water flowing south over flat land made the «river of grass»; no beavers live there | Right, beavers built them long ago | Alligators dug them | People dug them last year
test: Real life: how were the Himalayas made? | India's land pushed into Asia | An elephant dug them with its tusks long ago, as the oldest stories say | A volcano | The sea piled up sand
test: Real life: was the Sahara always a desert? | No: it was green with lakes until about 5,000 to 6,000 years ago | Yes, since the Earth began | It became a desert last century | It is not a desert
test: Real life: what are the dark patches on the Moon? | Plains of old cooled lava | A Fisherman and a Rat | Seas of water | Shadows of the Earth's mountains
test: Liam, little brother, shouting: «skibidi! the Rat ate the Moon!» (skibidi = a word kids shout for fun) What does the Rat really do in the story? | Bites the Fisherman's line | Eats the Moon bit by bit, which is why it gets thin each month | Pulls the sea | Swims to Bintang
test: Looking back: in the Armadillo story, who was told how to eat hedgehogs and tortoises? | Painted Jaguar, by his Mother | Pau Amma, by the Eldest Magician, at the Very Beginning | The Man | The Rat
test: Looking back: in the Whale story, which spot in the sea is «Fifty North and Forty West»? | Where the shipwrecked Mariner sat on his raft | Where Pau Amma lives | Where the Moon rises | Where Bintang is
test: Looking back: which earlier tale also explained how every animal of a kind got its look «from that day to this»? | The Rhinoceros story: every rhinoceros has folds | The First Letter story: every little girl has a beaver | Only the Alphabet story | None of them
test: Looking back: Taffy and Tegumai lived on Merrow Down in Surrey. Where is this story set? | Malaysia and the nearby seas | Surrey, by the river Wey, where the Tribe of Tegumai lived | The Amazon | Australia
test: ★★★ North, East, West, South. Which animal did the Magician visit THIRD? | The Beaver | The patient Cow | The old Turtle | The Elephant
test: ★★★ The Cow's «bare patch» and «the place where she had sat down» became two deserts. Which went with which, in the story's order? | Eaten patch: Indian Desert; sitting-place: Sahara | Eaten patch: Sahara; sitting-place: Indian Desert | Both became the Sahara | Neither: they became islands
test: ★★★ Careful: the Man says the sea comes up «once a day and once a night». How many times in one whole day and night does it come up the river? | Twice | Just once | Four times | Seven times
exam: Identify the creature which departed into the sea before receiving instruction as to its play. | Pau Amma the Crab | All-the-Turtle-there-was | The Rat of the Moon | All-the-Beaver-there-was
exam: Ascertain the geographical feature produced, according to the narrative, from the diggings of All-the-Elephant-there-was. | The Himalayan Mountains | The Desert of Sahara | The Everglades of Florida | The island of Java
exam: It is given that the Man complained of the Sea's conduct. Select the response most consistent with his complaint. | It ran up the river, flooding his house, and ran out again, leaving mud | It had swallowed his canoe | It had frozen solid | It had become salt
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of the Himalayas in the real world? | They were raised by the northward collision of the land of India with Asia | They were thrown up by an elephant | They were formed by wind-blown sand | They were built by people
